fields.instructions:
Read the lesson carefully.

Make sure you understand:
- the difference between for and while loops;
- how range() works;
- how a loop variable changes;
- why a while loop needs a condition that eventually becomes false.

When you finish reading, mark the activity as complete.

Read the Functions lesson carefully.

Make sure you understand:
- how to define a function with def;
- how to call a function;
- parameters and arguments;
- how return works;
- why functions are useful for reusable code.

When you finish reading, mark the activity as complete.

Read the Collections lesson carefully.

Make sure you understand:
- lists and indexing;
- tuples;
- sets and unique values;
- dictionaries and key-value pairs;
- how to loop through collections.

When you finish reading, mark the activity as complete.

Read the Object-Oriented Programming lesson carefully.

Make sure you understand:
- classes;
- objects;
- attributes;
- methods;
- the __init__() constructor;
- the self parameter.

Also make sure you understand the relationship between a class and the
objects created from it.

When you finish reading, mark the activity as complete.

Read the Introduction to Django lesson carefully.

Make sure you understand:
- what Django is;
- the difference between a project and an application;
- the purpose of manage.py;
- the roles of models, views, templates, and URLs;
- the basic Django request-response flow.

When you finish reading, mark the activity as complete.

Read the Django Project Setup and Structure lesson carefully.

Make sure you understand:
- what a Django project is;
- what manage.py is used for;
- the purpose of settings.py;
- the purpose of urls.py;
- how Django applications are created;
- why migrations are needed.

When you finish reading, mark the activity as complete.

Read the URL Routing and Views lesson carefully.

Make sure you understand:
- what a URL pattern is;
- how path() connects a URL to a view;
- what a Django view does;
- the difference between HttpResponse and render();
- how context data is passed to templates;
- how URL parameters work;
- why named URLs and include() are useful.

When you finish reading, mark the activity as complete.

Read the Templates and Static Files lesson carefully.

Make sure you understand:
- how Django renders templates;
- context variables;
- template tags;
- template filters;
- template inheritance;
- static files;
- {% load static %};
- collectstatic;
- CSRF tokens in POST forms.

When you finish reading, mark the activity as complete.

Read the Models and Database Basics lesson carefully.

Make sure you understand:
- what a Django model represents;
- common model field types;
- migrations;
- the Django ORM;
- QuerySets;
- create(), get(), filter(), save(), and delete();
- ForeignKey relationships.

When you finish reading, mark the activity as complete.

Read the Forms and User Input lesson carefully.

Make sure you understand:
- Django form classes;
- GET versus POST;
- CSRF protection;
- form validation;
- cleaned_data;
- validation errors;
- ModelForm;
- form widgets.

When you finish reading, mark the activity as complete.

Read the Authentication and Authorization lesson carefully.

Make sure you understand:
- authentication versus authorization;
- request.user;
- authenticate();
- login();
- logout();
- @login_required;
- staff and superuser users;
- permissions and groups;
- object ownership checks.

When you finish reading, mark the activity as complete.

Read the Django Admin and Application Management lesson carefully.

Make sure you understand:
- what Django Admin is;
- how models are registered;
- what ModelAdmin does;
- list_display;
- search_fields;
- list_filter;
- readonly_fields;
- inlines;
- staff and permission requirements.

When you finish reading, mark the activity as complete.

Read the Introduction to APIs and REST lesson carefully.

Make sure you understand:
- what an API is;
- what REST means;
- resources and endpoints;
- GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, and DELETE;
- common HTTP status codes;
- JSON;
- authentication versus permissions;
- what Django REST Framework provides.

When you finish reading, mark the activity as complete.

Read the DRF Project Setup and Configuration lesson carefully.

Make sure you understand:
- how djangorestframework is installed;
- why "rest_framework" is added to INSTALLED_APPS;
- how API URLs are configured;
- what APIView is;
- how as_view() connects an APIView to a URL;
- request.data;
- request.query_params;
- REST_FRAMEWORK settings;
- authentication and permissions.

When you finish reading, mark the activity as complete.

Read the Serializers and Data Validation lesson carefully.

Make sure you understand:
- serialization and deserialization;
- Serializer;
- ModelSerializer;
- serializer.data;
- serializer.is_valid();
- serializer.validated_data;
- serializer.errors;
- read_only_fields;
- custom validation;
- save() and partial updates.

When you finish reading, mark the activity as complete.

Read the Function-Based API Views lesson carefully.

Make sure you understand:
- @api_view;
- allowed HTTP methods;
- request.data;
- request.query_params;
- Response;
- HTTP status constants;
- permission_classes;
- URL parameters;
- serializers inside function-based API views.

When you finish reading, mark the activity as complete.

Read the Class-Based API Views and Generic Views lesson carefully.

Make sure you understand:
- APIView;
- class methods such as get() and post();
- as_view();
- generic API views;
- queryset;
- serializer_class;
- lookup_field;
- get_queryset();
- perform_create();
- ListAPIView;
- CreateAPIView;
- ListCreateAPIView;
- RetrieveUpdateDestroyAPIView.

When you finish reading, mark the activity as complete.

Read the ViewSets and Routers lesson carefully.

Make sure you understand:
- ViewSet;
- ModelViewSet;
- standard ViewSet actions;
- routers;
- router.register();
- serializer_class;
- queryset;
- lookup_field;
- @action;
- detail=True and detail=False;
- ViewSet permissions.

When you finish reading, mark the activity as complete.

Read the Authentication and Permissions lesson carefully.

Make sure you understand:
- authentication versus permissions;
- TokenAuthentication;
- Authorization headers;
- IsAuthenticated;
- AllowAny;
- IsAdminUser;
- IsAuthenticatedOrReadOnly;
- object-level ownership;
- custom permission classes;
- 401 versus 403;
- least privilege.

When you finish reading, mark the activity as complete.
